// @test: check
// @level: L2
// @tier: 0
// @tags: tensors, shapes, silent-acceptance
// @timeout: 30000
// @expected-exit: 101
// @expected-stderr: shape
// @ignore: shapes are not carried by the type — this file documents the
//   defect and will pass when they are. Measured 2026-09-03: `verum
//   check` is clean and `verum run` dies with `internal compiler error
//   (worker thread panicked): Expected int, got Some(0)`.
// @description: Adding tensors whose shapes cannot broadcast must be
//   refused at compile time, not crash the compiler at run time.

// `docs/language/tensor-types.md` promises "a failing broadcast is a
// compile error with the offending axis called out". It is not: the
// program below passes `verum check` with zero diagnostics.
//
// Its control — the same file with both shapes `tensor<2, 3>` — compiles
// AND runs, so this is a mismatch reaching the backend rather than a
// broken example. See `shape_match_is_accepted.vr`.
//
// The cause is one level down and invalidates the notation the public
// page uses throughout: `Tensor<Float32, [4, 3, 1]>` resolves to
// `DynTensor<Float32, [Int; 3]>` — rank survives, sizes do not. Only the
// literal form below yields a shape-bearing type, which is why the probe
// is written this way rather than with annotations.
//
// T1073.

public fn main() -> Int {
    let a = tensor<2, 3> Float32 { };
    let b = tensor<5, 4> Float32 { };

    // axis 0: 2 vs 5, axis 1: 3 vs 4 — neither is 1, so no broadcast.
    let c = a + b;

    0
}
